---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "By Goal",
    tags: [
      "best meal replacement shake",
      "meal replacement protein powder",
      "meal replacement shakes",
      "kachava alternatives"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Buyer's Guide"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Best Meal Replacement Shake</Fragment>
      Ka'Chava, Soylent, Ensure, Orgain and Garden of Life compared on the numbers that decide whether a shake is really a meal: calories, protein, fiber, micronutrients and what each one costs per meal, plus why Huel is not on the list.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#what-counts">What Makes a Meal Shake</a>
      <a href="#vs-protein">Meal Shake vs Protein Powder</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#compare">Side-by-Side Numbers</a>
      <a href="#calories">How Many Calories</a>
      <a href="#protein-fiber">Protein and Fiber</a>
      <a href="#micronutrients">Vitamins and Ingredients</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per Meal</a>
      <a href="#weight-loss">Meal Shakes for Weight Loss</a>
      <a href="#diy">Build Your Own</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          A meal replacement shake and a protein shake look alike in the glass and on the shelf, but they do different jobs. A protein powder gives you 20-30 g of protein in 100-150 calories and little else. A meal replacement adds carbohydrate, fat, fiber and a full vitamin and mineral premix, so one serving can stand in for breakfast or lunch. Ka'Chava is our top pick for most people, a 240-calorie superfood meal with about 25 g of plant protein and 6 g of fiber on its label. Soylent's 400-calorie bottle is the best full-size no-mix meal, Ensure Plus suits small appetites, Huel Black Edition is left out because Consumer Reports advised avoiding it over lead, and if you only wanted protein, a plain powder costs a fraction of any of them.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-counts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Makes a Shake a Meal Replacement
          </h2>

          <p>
            In the US, meal replacement is a marketing term rather than a tightly defined category, so the words on the bag tell you very little. Anything from a 150-calorie protein-and-greens powder to a 400-calorie bottle of soy, oil and maltodextrin can be sold as a meal. The only reliable way to know what you are buying is to read the nutrition panel and ask whether it could plausibly do the job of the meal you plan to skip.
          </p>

          <p>
            A shake that can genuinely replace a meal has four things. First, enough energy, usually somewhere between 250 and 500 calories, to keep an adult going for a few hours. Second, a meaningful dose of protein, ideally 20 g or more, because protein does most of the work of keeping you full. Third, fiber, preferably 5 g or more, which slows digestion and supports gut health. Fourth, a broad vitamin and mineral premix, so that skipping a meal does not mean skipping the nutrients that meal would have provided.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most of the shakes people search for by name pass that test to different degrees. Soylent's bottle lands at about 400 calories and Ensure Plus at about 350, which is a real meal for most people. Ka'Chava sits at about 240 calories, a light breakfast. Garden of Life Raw Organic Meal lists just 150 calories, which is a snack unless you add food to it. All of them carry added vitamins and minerals; the differences come down to calories, protein, fiber and how much you pay for each serving.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein shakes sold in the same aisle usually fail at least two of the four tests. A Premier Protein carton or a scoop of whey gives you 25-30 g of protein in 110-160 calories with little fiber or fat. That makes them excellent at what they are for, topping up protein between meals, and poor at standing in for lunch. If you drink one instead of a meal, most people end up hungry within an hour or two and eat more later.
          </p>

          <p>
            So before choosing a brand, decide which job you are hiring the shake for. If you want to skip cooking breakfast and still eat properly, you want a meal shake. If you want more protein in a day that already has three meals, you want a protein powder, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder/">best protein powder</a> guide is the better place to start. Buying the wrong one is the single most common and expensive mistake in this category.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Meal Replacement vs Protein Powder
          </h2>

          <p>
            The clearest way to see the difference is to line the two up per serving. A typical meal shake roughly triples the calories of a protein shake, adds carbohydrate and fat, and brings in fiber and a multivitamin's worth of micronutrients. A protein powder concentrates the protein and strips almost everything else out. Neither is better in the abstract; they are different tools.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Per serving</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical meal shake</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical protein powder</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">240-400</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100-150</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20-40 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20-30 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fiber</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-7 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0-3 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vitamins and minerals</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Full premix, often 25+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually none or a few</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cost per serving</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1.50-4.65 dollars</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often about 1-2 dollars</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Look at protein per calorie and the gap is stark. Ka'Chava gives about 10 g of protein per 100 calories, which is good for a meal shake. Soylent's bottle and Ensure Plus give about 5 g per 100 calories, and the Orgain Organic Nutrition carton about 6.4 g. A whey isolate commonly gives 20-25 g per 100 calories. If you are counting protein against a calorie budget, the protein powder wins by a wide margin, and that matters for anyone in a calorie deficit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Now look at fullness and nutrition, and the meal shake wins. The extra carbohydrate, fat and fiber slow digestion, so a 400-calorie meal shake keeps most people satisfied far longer than a 120-calorie protein shake. The vitamin and mineral premix also means a meal shake can replace a balanced meal without leaving a nutrient gap, something a protein powder was never designed to do.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also a middle path that many buyers end up on. A protein powder blended with oats, fruit, nut butter or milk becomes a meal shake you design yourself, usually for less money and with more control over sugar and fiber. We cover that in the last section, and it is why the final pick on our list is a plain protein powder rather than another branded meal.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Best Meal Replacement Shakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            We ranked these on the label, not on taste tests: calories per serving, protein per serving, fiber, the breadth of the vitamin and mineral premix, and cost per meal at prices we saw at the time of writing. We did not test these products hands-on. Figures are per the label or the brand's listing and vary by flavor, and prices move constantly, so check the panel and the price on the product you actually buy. We excluded Huel Black Edition, which Consumer Reports advised avoiding over lead, as explained below. The last pick is a protein powder, included for readers who realise halfway down that a protein shake was what they needed all along.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="compare" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Side-by-Side: Calories, Protein, Fiber and Cost
          </h2>

          <p>
            Here are the five meal shakes on our list, plus the DIY protein base, compared on the numbers that matter. Figures are per the label or brand listings at the time of writing and vary by flavor. Costs are approximate, based on the prices we saw, and change with subscriptions, multipacks and sales, so treat them as a guide to relative value rather than a quote.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Shake</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Fiber</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Approx. cost per meal</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ka'Chava</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 240</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 6 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 4.00-4.65 dollars</td>
                  </tr>
